          "description": "Expressing milestones and completed future deadlines in passive voice using will/shall + have + been + V3.",
          "duration": "25 mins",
          "type": "text",
          "content": "# 🔵 Future Perfect Tense in Passive Voice\n\n> **Rule:** `Object + will / shall + have + been + Past Participle (V3) + (by + Subject)`\n\n---\n\n### 📖 Introduction & Concept\nFuture Perfect Tense indicates an action that will be completed prior to a designated point or deadline in the future. In passive voice, we combine **will have been** with the **past participle (V3)** of the main verb.\n\n#### Crucial Time Markers:\nFuture Perfect sentences almost always contain explicit deadline markers:\n- *by 5 PM, by next Friday, before the month ends, by the time you arrive*\n\n---\n\n### 📊 Sentence Structure Breakdown\n\n| Sentence Type | Active Voice Formula | Passive Voice Formula |\n|---|---|---|\n| **✅ Positive** | `Subject + will/shall + have + V3 + Object` | `Object + will/shall + have been + V3 + by + Subject` |\n| **❌ Negative** | `Subject + will/shall + not + have + V3 + Object` | `Object + will/shall + not + have been + V3 + by + Subject` |\n| **❓ Interrogative** | `Will/Shall + Subject + have + V3 + Object + ?` | `Will/Shall + Object + have been + V3 + by + Subject + ?` |\n| **❓➕ Interrog. Negative** | `Will/Shall + Subject + not + have + V3 + Object + ?` | `Will/Shall + Object + not + have been + V3 + by + Subject + ?` |\n\n---\n\n### 📌 Practical Examples\n\n#### ✅ Positive Conversions\n- **Active:** The contractor will have completed the mega dam by 2028.\n- **Passive:** The mega dam will have been completed by the contractor by 2028.\n- **Active:** She will have compiled the statistical report before the conference begins.\n- **Passive:** The statistical report will have been compiled by her before the conference begins.\n\n#### ❌ Negative Conversions\n- **Active:** The technicians will not have restored internet connectivity by noon.\n- **Passive:** Internet connectivity will not have been restored by the technicians by noon.\n- **Active:** They will not have harvested the crop before the monsoon rains start.\n- **Passive:** The crop will not have been harvested by them before the monsoon rains start.\n\n#### ❓ Interrogative Conversions\n- **Active:** Will the university have declared the examination results before September?\n- **Passive:** Will the examination results have been declared by the university before September?\n- **Active:** Will the mechanics have repaired the aircraft before takeoff?\n- **Passive:** Will the aircraft have been repaired by the mechanics before takeoff?\n\n#### ❓➕ Interrogative Negative Conversions\n- **Active:** Will the publisher not have printed the textbooks before the academic term starts?\n- **Passive:** Will the textbooks not have been printed by the publisher before the academic term starts?\n- **Active:** Won't they have verified the credentials before issuing the visa?\n- **Passive:** Won't the credentials have been verified by them before issuing the visa?\n\n---\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes & Pitfalls\n\n- ❌ **Wrong:** *The assignment will have finished by June.*\n  - ✅ **Right:** *The assignment **will have been finished** by June.* (Without 'been', the assignment appears to finish itself).\n- ❌ **Wrong:** *The files will has been audited.*\n  - ✅ **Right:** *The files **will have been** audited.* (After modal 'will', always use base 'have', never 'has').\n- ❌ **Wrong:** *Will have been the road completed by next year?*\n  - ✅ **Right:** ***Will the road have been** completed by next year?*\n",
