          "id": "les-14-15",
          "title": "Imperative Sentences (Orders, Requests, Advice & Suggestions)",
          "description": "Transforming commands, prohibitions, polite requests, and moral advice using Let + be and You are requested/ordered to.",
          "duration": "25 mins",
          "type": "text",
          "content": "# ⚡ Imperative Sentences in Passive Voice\n\n> **Command / Order Formula:** `Let + Object + be + Past Participle (V3)`\n> **Moral Advice Formula:** `Object + should be + Past Participle (V3)`\n> **Request / Explicit Formula:** `You are requested / ordered / advised + to + V1`\n\n---\n\n### 📖 Introduction & Concept\nImperative sentences express orders, instructions, requests, advice, warnings, or prohibitions. Unlike assertive statements, imperatives typically lack an explicit grammatical subject because the second person (\"You\") is implied (*\"Open the door\"* implies *\"You open the door\"*).\n\n#### Three Strategies for Transforming Imperatives:\n\n1. **Standard Commands (Using \"Let\"):**\n   - *Active:* \"Shut the window.\"\n   - *Passive:* \"Let the window be shut.\"\n\n2. **Moral Advice or Obligation (Using \"Should be\"):**\n   - *Active:* \"Help the poor.\"\n   - *Passive:* \"The poor should be helped.\" (Alternatively: *\"Let the poor be helped\"*).\n\n3. **Intransitive Verbs / Explicit Politeness (Using \"You are ordered/requested to\"):**\n   - *Active:* \"Please sit down.\"\n   - *Passive:* \"You are requested to sit down.\"\n   - *Active:* \"Work hard.\"\n   - *Passive:* \"You are advised to work hard.\"\n\n---\n\n### 📊 Structural Matrix for Imperatives\n\n| Imperative Category | Active Structure | Passive Structure |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Orders / Commands (Transitive)** | `V1 + Object` | `Let + Object + be + V3` |\n| **Prohibitions (Negative Orders)** | `Do not + V1 + Object` | `Let + Object + not be + V3` |\n| **Moral Advice / Duty** | `V1 + Object` | `Object + should be + V3` |\n| **Polite Requests (with 'Please')** | `Please / Kindly + V1 + ...` | `You are requested to + V1 + ...` |\n| **Intransitive Commands** | `V1 (+ Adverb/Preposition)` | `You are ordered/told to + V1 + ...` |\n\n---\n\n### 📌 Practical Examples\n\n#### ✅ Commands & Orders\n- **Active:** Post this urgent letter immediately.\n- **Passive:** Let this urgent letter be posted immediately.\n- **Active:** Switch off all electrical appliances before leaving.\n- **Passive:** Let all electrical appliances be switched off before leaving.\n\n#### ❌ Negative Imperatives (Prohibitions)\n- **Active:** Do not pluck the flowers in the public park.\n- **Passive:** Let the flowers in the public park not be plucked.\n- **Active:** Do not insult the hardworking laborer.\n- **Passive:** Let the hardworking laborer not be insulted. (Or: *The hardworking laborer should not be insulted.*)\n\n#### 🤝 Moral Advice & Recommendations\n- **Active:** Respect your elders and teachers.\n- **Passive:** Your elders and teachers should be respected.\n- **Active:** Never tell a lie.\n- **Passive:** A lie should never be told. (Or: *Let a lie never be told.*)\n\n#### 🙏 Polite Requests\n- **Active:** Please grant me three days leave of absence.\n- **Passive:** You are requested to grant me three days leave of absence.\n- **Active:** Kindly review my resume for the position.\n- **Passive:** You are kindly requested to review my resume for the position.\n\n#### 🚶 Intransitive Imperatives\n- **Active:** Stand up on the bench!\n- **Passive:** You are ordered to stand up on the bench.\n- **Active:** Walk carefully on the icy road.\n- **Passive:** You are advised to walk carefully on the icy road.\n\n---\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes & Pitfalls\n\n- ❌ **Wrong:** *Let the door opened.*\n  - ✅ **Right:** *Let the door **be opened**.* ('Be' is mandatory after the object).\n- ❌ **Wrong:** *You are requested to please help me.*\n  - ✅ **Right:** *You are requested to help me.* (Do not repeat 'please' when 'requested' is present).\n- ❌ **Wrong:** *Let not do this.*\n  - ✅ **Right:** *Let this **not be done**.*\n",
