          "id": "les-14-17",
          "title": "Verbs with Two Objects & Prepositional Verbs",
          "description": "Direct vs Indirect objects (dative shift) and phrasal/prepositional verbs that retain their fixed prepositions.",
          "duration": "25 mins",
          "type": "text",
          "content": "# 🧩 Verbs with Two Objects & Prepositional Verbs\n\n> **Direct vs Indirect Object:** Either object can become the new subject.\n> **Fixed Preposition Rule:** Never drop the verb's accompanying preposition in passive voice.\n\n---\n\n### 📖 Part 1: Verbs with Double Objects (Ditransitive Verbs)\nCertain verbs (*give, send, teach, show, tell, offer, pay, lend, grant*) can take **two objects**:\n1. **Direct Object (DO):** The thing acted upon (*e.g., a story, a scholarship, a gift*).\n2. **Indirect Object (IO):** The person receiving the direct object (*e.g., me, her, the student*).\n\n#### Two Valid Passive Options:\n- *Active:* \"The committee awarded Dr Wazir the gold medal.\"\n  - **Option A (Indirect Object as Subject - Highly Preferred in English):**\n    *\"Dr Wazir was awarded the gold medal by the committee.\"*\n  - **Option B (Direct Object as Subject - Requires 'to' before the person):**\n    *\"The gold medal was awarded to Dr Wazir by the committee.\"*\n\n*Note: Native English speakers overwhelmingly prefer using the person (Indirect Object) as the subject of the passive sentence.*\n\n---\n\n### 📖 Part 2: Prepositional & Phrasal Verbs\nWhen a verb forms a fixed unit with a preposition (*laugh at, look after, care for, break into, send for, listen to*), **the preposition must never be dropped in passive voice**. It stays glued directly to the past participle.\n\n- *Active:* \"The students laughed at the jester.\"\n  - ✅ *Passive:* \"The jester was laughed **at** by the students.\" (Both 'at' and 'by' are correct and necessary).\n  - ❌ *Incorrect:* *\"The jester was laughed by the students.\"* (Grammatically incorrect; loses meaning).\n\n---\n\n### 📊 Structural Comparison Matrix\n\n| Verb Type | Active Voice | Passive Voice (Form 1) | Passive Voice (Form 2) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Two Objects** | `Sub + Verb + IO + DO` | `IO + be + V3 + DO + by + Sub` | `DO + be + V3 + to/for + IO + by + Sub` |\n| **Prepositional Verb** | `Sub + Verb + Prep + Obj` | `Obj + be + V3 + Prep + by + Sub` | — |\n\n---\n\n### 📌 Practical Examples\n\n#### 🎁 Double Objects\n- **Active:** My father bought me a new laptop.\n  - **Passive 1:** I was bought a new laptop by my father.\n  - **Passive 2:** A new laptop was bought **for** me by my father.\n- **Active:** The professor taught us complex engineering principles.\n  - **Passive 1:** We were taught complex engineering principles by the professor.\n  - **Passive 2:** Complex engineering principles were taught **to** us by the professor.\n\n#### 🔗 Prepositional & Phrasal Verbs\n- **Active:** The nurse is looking after the elderly patient.\n- **Passive:** The elderly patient is being looked **after** by the nurse.\n- **Active:** Burglars broke into the jewelry shop at midnight.\n- **Passive:** The jewelry shop was broken **into** by burglars at midnight.\n- **Active:** You must listen to your parents' advice.\n- **Passive:** Your parents' advice must be listened **to** by you.\n- **Active:** The general sent for the battalion commander immediately.\n- **Passive:** The battalion commander was sent **for** immediately by the general.\n\n---\n\n### ⚠️ Common Mistakes & Pitfalls\n\n- ❌ **Wrong:** *The proposal was agreed by the members.*\n  - ✅ **Right:** *The proposal was agreed **to** by the members.* (Agree requires 'to').\n- ❌ **Wrong:** *He was given a pen to.*\n  - ✅ **Right:** *A pen was given **to him**.* OR *He was given a pen.*\n- ❌ **Wrong:** *The orphan was looked by his aunt.*\n  - ✅ **Right:** *The orphan was looked **after** by his aunt.*\n",
