      "content": "# 🔵 Future Perfect Continuous: Passive Analysis\n\n> **Theoretical Formula:** `Object + will + have + been + being + Past Participle (V3) + (by + Subject)`\n\n---\n\n### 📖 Introduction & Conventional Usage\nFuture Perfect Continuous describes an ongoing duration leading up to a specific milestone in the future. In passive voice, **it is universally recognized as non-standard and avoided in English writing and discourse**.\n\n#### Why is it Avoided?\nNotice the triple-auxiliary stack: **will have been being + V3**:\n- *Active:* \"By next August, Dr Wazir will have been mentoring researchers for 25 years.\"\n- *Theoretical Passive:* \"By next August, researchers **will have been being mentored** by Dr Wazir for 25 years.\"\n\nThis sentence is unwieldy, confusing, and practically impossible to pronounce smoothly.\n\n#### Elegant Solutions:\n1. **Keep Active Voice (Recommended):** *\"By next August, Dr Wazir will have been mentoring researchers for 25 years.\"*\n2. **Convert to Future Perfect Passive:** *\"By next August, hundreds of researchers will have been mentored by Dr Wazir.\"*\n\n---\n\n### 📊 Theoretical Structural Matrix\n\n| Sentence Type | Theoretical Passive Structure | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| **✅ Positive** | `Object + will have been being + V3` | The highway **will have been being repaired** for a month by June. |\n| **❌ Negative** | `Object + will not have been being + V3` | The bridge **will not have been being used** for long. |\n| **❓ Interrogative** | `Will + Object + have been being + V3 + ?` | **Will** the experiment **have been being monitored** for 24 hours? |\n| **❓➕ Interrog. Negative** | `Will + Object + not + have been being + V3 + ?` | **Will** the facility **not have been being guarded** by security? |\n\n---\n\n### ⚠️ Summary of the 4 Non-Passive Tenses in English\nThe following four tenses do not customarily form passive voice in standard English:\n1. **Present Perfect Continuous** (*has/have been being*)\n2. **Past Perfect Continuous** (*had been being*)\n3. **Future Continuous** (*will be being*)\n4. **Future Perfect Continuous** (*will have been being*)\n",
